--------------------------------------- Templates ---------------------------------------------------

- LEARN A BIT MORE ABOUT COMPONENTS AND THE TEMPLATING SYNTAX WITH A NEW COMPONENT FOR THE RESPONSIVE
    MENU.

- All the required files can be found inside the resource3 folder:

- Instructions:

1️⃣ Let’s add our first component to the application: the menu. The main page will contain a classical
    navigation menu allowing to go back to the main page, and to access the various new features that
    we will develop.
    To create a new component, you can of course do it manually by creating a new directory and adding
    the component files, or you can use Angular CLI:

        ng generate component menu
    
    A new folder menu appears in the app folder. It contains the TypeScript file defining the component,
    as well as its HTML template and its CSS stylesheet. A test file is also generated. We’ll talk
    about it later.


2️⃣ Let’s create the template of our menu component. Since this is a standard Bootstrap navbar, you can
    look at the Bootstrap documentation for the necessary markup. To make it simpler, and since it’s
    not really Angular-related, here it is:

    menu.html:

        <nav class="navbar navbar-expand-md navbar-light bg-light">
            <div class="container-fluid">
                <a class="navbar-brand">PonyRacer</a>
                <div id="navbar" class="navbar-collapse">
                    <ul class="navbar-nav me-auto">
                        <li class="nav-item">
                            <a class="nav-link">Races</a>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </nav>


3️⃣ To go further, we need to learn a bit more about the Angular template syntax.
    Our menu doesn’t do much right now. We haven’t even included it in the application. Let’s fix that.
    Open the template of the main component, app.html, and add the required markup to display our Menu
    at the very top (above the main tag).
    🔥Angular will recognize the tag <pr-menu> as the selector of the Angular component Menu only if
    we add the Menu to the imports field of the App.
    ✨Refresh the page, the menu should appear in your browser!


4️⃣ This menu isn’t responsive yet. If you reduce the page size to the dimension it would have on a
    phone, it would be nice to have a button appearing on the right side of the menu, allowing to
    display a list of the items in the menu. Let’s use our new templating skills to achieve that.

    First step: add a button in the header of the navbar:

    menu.html:

        <nav class="navbar navbar-expand-md navbar-light bg-light">
            <div class="container-fluid">
                <a class="navbar-brand">PonyRacer</a>
                <button type="button" class="navbar-toggler">
                    <span class="navbar-toggler-icon"></span>
                </button>
                <div id="navbar" class="navbar-collapse">
                    <ul class="navbar-nav me-auto">
                        <li class="nav-item">
                            <a class="nav-link">Races</a>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </nav>
    
    Now, if you reduce the width of your browser window, the button will appear on its own. Cool!


5️⃣ But it still doesn’t do anything when clicked. We would like to make the menu visible when we
    click the button.
    🧠The way to do that, using Bootstrap, is to dynamically add the CSS class collapse to the div
    identified by navbar. This CSS class must be added when we click the button, and should be
    removed if we click it a second time (to hide the menu).
    Now, the old-fashioned way to do that would be to modify the DOM. But that’s not how Angular
    works. With Angular, the DOM modifies itself based on the state of the component.
    
    We thus have to:

    👉add a signal navbarCollapsed in the component, initialized to true (the menu is collapsed by
    default);

    👉add a click event handler on the button. This handler should call a method toggleNavbar() of
    our Menu component;

    👉write this method, and make it flip the value of the navbarCollapsed signal;

    👉use [class.collapse] in the template in order to dynamically add or remove the CSS class
    collapse to/from the div with the id navbar based on the value of the navbarCollapsed signal.


6️⃣ Now let’s verify everything has been done correctly. You can of course test it in your browser,
    but ✨we will automate this verification by using the provided unit tests menu.spec.ts and
    app.spec.ts. We will learn later how to write such unit tests. For the time being, you just
    need to replace the files in your project by the one provided with this exercise, and to
    execute the following command:

        ng test
    
    If you did it right, the test should all pass.  If you didn’t, the result might look like the
    following:

        Chrome 52.0.2743 (Mac OS X 10.11.6) Menu Component should have a `navbarCollapsed` field FAILED
        Expected undefined to be true, 'Check that `navbarCollapsed` is initialized with `true`.
        Maybe you forgot to declare `navbarCollapsed` in your component.'.
    
    That means that the tests were executed, and that at least one of them failed. The first line
    in this example output shows that the test "Menu Component should have a navbarCollapsed field"
    has failed, because the test expected a value true and instead got a value undefined for the
    field navbarCollapsed. Additionally, it displays a hint explaining how you (probably) messed up:

        Check that `navbarCollapsed` is initialized with `true`. Maybe you forgot to declare
        `navbarCollapsed` in your component.


- 🎯Your goal is to make all the tests pass, in order to validate this exercise

- 🧠You can run the tests automatically every time you save your changes by running the command
    ng test.